  "topics": [
    {
      "id": "pronunciation",
      "title": "Alphabet & pronunciation",
      "fi": "Aakkoset ja ääntäminen",
      "emoji": "🔤",
      "summary": "Finnish is written almost exactly as it is spoken: one letter = one sound, and every letter is pronounced.",
      "sections": [
        {
          "heading": "The golden rule",
          "body": "Each letter always stands for the same sound, and **every letter is pronounced** — there are no silent letters. If you can read a word aloud letter by letter, you can pronounce it. The reverse is also true: if you hear a word clearly, you can spell it.",
          "examples": [
            {
              "fi": "talo",
              "en": "house — t-a-l-o"
            },
            {
              "fi": "kahvi",
              "en": "coffee — the h is pronounced"
            },
            {
              "fi": "keittiö",
              "en": "kitchen — k-e-i-t-t-i-ö"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "The alphabet (aakkoset)",
          "body": "The Finnish alphabet has 29 letters. Native words use only 21 of them; b, c, f, g (except in ng), q, š, w, x, z and å appear in loanwords and names.",
