    {
      "id": "long-short",
      "title": "Long and short sounds",
      "fi": "Pitkät ja lyhyet äänteet",
      "emoji": "⏱️",
      "summary": "A doubled letter is a longer sound — and length changes the meaning of the word.",
      "requires": [
        "pronunciation"
      ],
      "sections": [
        {
          "heading": "Length is meaning",
          "body": "Every vowel and most consonants can be short (one letter) or long (two letters). A long sound is held roughly twice as long. Getting the length wrong gives you a different word, so practise hearing it.",
          "table": {
            "headers": [
              "Short",
              "Long vowel",
              "Long consonant"
            ],
            "rows": [
              [
                "tuli — fire",
                "tuuli — wind",
                "tulli — customs"
              ],
              [
                "muta — mud",
                "muuta — other (partitive)",
                "mutta — but"
              ],
              [
                "kisa — contest",
                "kiisu — kitty",
                "kissa — cat"
              ],
              [
                "sika — pig",
                "siika — whitefish",
                "—"
              ],
              [
                "tapan — I kill",
                "—",
                "tappaa — he/she kills"
              ],
              [
                "kansa — people",
                "—",
                "kanssa — with"
              ]
            ]
          }
        },
        {
          "heading": "How to pronounce long consonants",
          "body": "A double consonant (**kk, pp, tt, ss, ll, mm, nn, rr**) is a real pause/hold in the middle of the word, belonging to both syllables: *kis-sa*, *mat-to*, *kauppa* (kaup-pa). Think of the gap in English 'black cat' or 'hot tea'."
        },
        {
          "heading": "Minimal pairs from the course",
          "examples": [
            {
              "fi": "tuli / tuuli / tulli",
              "en": "fire / wind / customs"
            },
            {
              "fi": "mutta / muuta",
              "en": "but / other (partitive)"
            },
            {
              "fi": "kuka / kukka",
              "en": "who / flower"
            },
            {
              "fi": "taka / takka",
              "en": "back- / fireplace"
            },
            {
              "fi": "pala / palaa / pallo",
              "en": "piece / burns / ball"
            },
            {
              "fi": "sää / saa",
              "en": "weather / gets (vowel quality, not length)"
            },
            {
              "fi": "tyyli / tyyni",
              "en": "style / calm"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "Why it matters for grammar",
          "body": "Length is also how grammar works: the hän form of a type 1 verb lengthens the last vowel (*puhu-* → **puhuu**), the illative case lengthens the vowel (*talo* → **taloon**), and K-P-T gradation turns long consonants short (*kauppa* → *kaupassa*). See [[kpt-nouns|Consonant gradation in nouns]] and [[kpt-verbs|K-P-T in verbs]]."
        }
      ],
