    {
      "id": "stress-diphthongs",
      "title": "Stress & diphthongs",
      "fi": "Paino ja diftongit",
      "emoji": "🎵",
      "summary": "Stress always falls on the first syllable. Two different vowels side by side often glide together into one syllable (a diphthong).",
      "requires": [
        "pronunciation"
      ],
      "sections": [
        {
          "heading": "Stress: always the first syllable",
          "body": "The main stress is **always on the first syllable**, no matter how long the word is. In long words there is a lighter secondary stress on the 3rd or 5th syllable. Long vowels in later syllables are *not* stressed — they are just long.",
          "examples": [
            {
              "fi": "SUO-mi",
              "en": "Finland"
            },
            {
              "fi": "HEL-sin-ki",
              "en": "Helsinki"
            },
            {
              "fi": "RA-vin-to-la",
              "en": "restaurant"
            },
            {
              "fi": "KAU-pas-sa",
              "en": "in the shop"
            },
            {
              "fi": "TA-loon",
              "en": "into the house (long oo, stress still on ta-)"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "Diphthongs",
          "body": "A diphthong is two different vowels pronounced in one syllable, gliding from the first to the second. Pronounce both vowels clearly.",
          "table": {
            "headers": [
              "Ending in",
              "Diphthongs",
              "Examples"
            ],
            "rows": [
              [
                "-i",
                "ai, ei, oi, ui, yi, äi, öi",
                "kaikki, hei, poika, uida, äiti, öisin"
              ],
              [
                "-u / -y",
                "au, eu, iu, ou / äy, öy",
                "auto, Eurooppa, kiuas, Oulu / käydä, löytää"
              ],
              [
                "opening (first syllable only)",
                "ie, uo, yö",
                "tie, Suomi, yö"
              ]
            ]
          },
          "note": "ie, uo and yö are only diphthongs in the first syllable; elsewhere the two vowels belong to separate syllables: *ra-di-o*, *tor-ni-en*."
        },
        {
          "heading": "Practice words",
          "examples": [
            {
              "fi": "yö — työ — Töölö",
              "en": "night — work — (district of Helsinki)"
            },
            {
